@@s001 Why concurrency
@@s002 <p><b>Sequential</b>: one core, one task after the other. Task 2 waits for task 1 to finish.</p>
@@s003 <p><b>Parallel</b>: two cores, the two tasks advance <b>at the same instant</b>. Total time halved, if the tasks are independent.</p>
@@s004 <p><b>Concurrent</b>: the computation is split into blocks and the blocks of the two tasks <b>alternate</b>, even on a single core. The scheduler decides the order of the blocks. With more cores, some blocks can also run in parallel.</p>
@@s005 dato:person 1,due:person 2,no:waiting or getting in the way
@@s006 dato:inherent,due:hidden,ora:exploitation,ok:the example
@@s007 dato:Goetz,due:Nygaard,ok:an example placed
@@s008 Why concurrency
@@s009 Concurrency means <b>doing more than one computation in the same period of time</b>. Today almost every system uses it: websites, interfaces, operating systems, streaming, machine learning models. This page clarifies the words and the reasons, including the comparison of exercise 1.4.
@@s010 The question of the ditch
@@s011 Lecture 1 opens with a riddle (slide 15) that already contains the two questions of the whole course. More workers finish sooner only if the work <b>can be divided</b> and if they do not have to <b>share</b> the tools. With computers it is the same: more threads or more cores help only if the problem splits into independent parts, and the shared resources (a variable, a file, the memory) must be coordinated, at a cost.
@@s012 Do two people dig a ditch faster than one? (slide 15)
@@s013 One person digs 4 metres of ditch in 2 hours. How long do two people take?
@@s014 The instinctive answer: 1 hour. The work is divided.
@@s015 …and if they have <mark class="no">only one spade</mark>?
@@s016 A <b>shared resource</b>: they must take turns. No gain, indeed time is lost passing it on.
@@s017 …and if they must dig <mark class="no">a hole</mark> instead of a ditch?
@@s018 Not all work divides: if they work in the same spot they get in each other's way.
@@s019 And 100 people? And 100 PCs that sort 4 billion numbers <mark class="due">reading from the same memory</mark>?
@@s020 The same questions for computers: the shared resources and the type of problem decide how much it helps to have many. That is what the course is about.
@@s021 One spade or two, a ditch or a hole
@@s022 Sequential, parallel, concurrent
@@s023 Two tasks on the timeline
@@s024 Blocks of computation
@@s025 Is more than one core needed?
@@s026 <b>Sequential</b>
@@s027 one whole task after the other
@@s028 no
@@s029 <b>Parallel</b>
@@s030 the tasks advance together
@@s031 yes
@@s032 <b>Concurrent</b>
@@s033 the computation is split into arbitrary blocks that mix
@@s034 no (but with more cores some blocks run in parallel)
@@s035 Abstract and concrete
@@s036 Concurrency is an <b>abstract</b> concept: several flows of instructions (<i>streams</i>) plus a way of coordinating them. In practice you implement it with <b>concrete</b> tools: in Java threads, locks, monitors, semaphores; in Erlang processes and messages. The course teaches the concepts using Java.
@@s037 The three reasons, according to Nygaard
@@s038 The course notes use the classification of the Norwegian Kristen Nygaard.
@@s039 Inherent (intrinsic)
@@s040 The problem <b>is already</b> made of several independent flows: user interfaces, input/output. An app must read the GPS while you download a page and touch the screen.
@@s041 Exploitation
@@s042 The hardware can run several flows <b>together</b> and you want to use it: more cores, GPUs, different computers coordinated over the network.
@@s043 Hidden (hidden, virtual)
@@s044 Several programs <b>share</b> the same resources and each behaves as if it had them all to itself: the operating system that runs many programs on a few cores.
@@s045 Requesting a web page from a server: what kind of concurrency is it? (slide 28)
@@s046 Your browser waits for the answer <mark class="dato">while</mark> remaining usable.
@@s047 <b>Inherent</b>: the user's input and the network are independent flows.
@@s048 The server answers thousands of users <mark class="due">on the same cores</mark>.
@@s049 <b>Hidden</b>: each request behaves as if the server were its own.
@@s050 The server uses <mark class="ora">all the cores</mark> to serve more per second.
@@s051 <b>Exploitation</b>.
@@s052 <mark class="ok">All three.</mark>
@@s053 The categories do not exclude each other: the same real system often has more than one.
@@s054 Inherent, hidden, exploitation
@@s055 Goetz against Nygaard (exercise 1.4)
@@s056 Goetz (ch. 1.1) gives three other reasons. The comparison asks for examples that fall into one classification and not the other.
@@s057 Goetz
@@s058 Idea
@@s059 Close to Nygaard…
@@s060 <b>Resource utilization</b>
@@s061 do not leave the CPU idle while a program waits for I/O
@@s062 exploitation / hidden
@@s063 <b>Fairness</b>
@@s064 several users or programs share the machine, each gets its part
@@s065 hidden
@@s066 <b>Convenience</b>
@@s067 it is more convenient to write many small programs that coordinate than one enormous one
@@s068 <b>no</b> precise category
@@s069 Doubt: how do I find an example that falls only on one side?
@@s070 Look at <b>the reason</b> why the system is concurrent, not the system itself.
@@s071 <b>Only Goetz</b>: a closed simulation, without I/O and on a single core, split into threads <b>only because it is more convenient to write</b> (convenience). There is no I/O (no inherent), no extra hardware is exploited (no exploitation), it shares no resources with other programs (no hidden).
@@s072 <b>Only Nygaard</b>: one possible argument is a computation coordinated among many independent computers via the internet (exploitation). Goetz's three reasons are meant for programs that share one machine; coordinating different computers is not among its examples.
@@s073 The text itself says that the definitions are vague and that many answers are accepted: what counts is <b>arguing</b> with the reason.
@@s074 In English
@@s075 "A closed simulation split into threads only to structure the code is concurrent for convenience, which is one of Goetz's motivations, but it fits none of Nygaard's categories: there is no inherent I/O, no hardware being exploited and no resource sharing with other programs."
@@s076 Examples from one side only
